keep-data: the old system Debian GNU/Linux 12 (bookworm) (old.example.net) on /dev/mapper/vg0-root; UEFI firmware; the layout of the profile is not applied, no disk is wiped or partitioned
FORMAT (what is on them is lost)
  /dev/mapper/vg0-root  ext4  /  (10G)
  /dev/vda2             ext4  /boot  (1G)
KEEP (as they are, mounted where they were)
  /dev/vda1             vfat  /boot/efi  (the EFI system partition: not formatted, the new boot loader is added)
  /dev/vda4             ext4  /home  (encrypted: opened by the new system as home, with a passphrase (nofail))
UNTOUCHED (not in the old fstab: left as they are)
  /dev/mapper/vg0-data  xfs   10G
  /dev/mapper/vg0-swap  swap  1G
  /dev/mapper/vg0-var   ext4  5G
LOST (on the old root, beyond the system itself)
  /backup  2M
  /etc  6M
  /home/alice  1.1G
  /home/bob  1M
  /opt  245M
  /root  1M
  /usr/local  69M
  /var/lib/apt  176M
  /var/lib/docker  8.5G
PACKAGES (installed for what is kept): cryptsetup
WARNINGS
  the encrypted volume home asks for a passphrase: nofail in crypttab and fstab, so that the new system boots without it; open it after the boot (systemctl start systemd-cryptsetup@home)
  /home is kept and not mounted by the installer (encrypted): the profile's users' home directories and keys are made on the root, where it hides them once it is mounted
  the profile's users' keys are also in /etc/ssh/authorized_keys/NAME, which the new system's sshd reads besides (sshd_config.d/20-ump-keep-data.conf): they get in whatever /home holds
--- /etc/fstab
# /etc/fstab, written by UMP (keep-data: the root made anew, the old system's other volumes as they were)
UUID=NEW-vg0-root  /  ext4  defaults,errors=remount-ro  0 1
UUID=NEW-vda2  /boot  ext4  defaults  0 2
UUID=769B-CE7F  /boot/efi  vfat  umask=0077  0 1
/dev/mapper/home  /home  ext4  defaults,nofail  0 2
--- /etc/crypttab
# /etc/crypttab, written by UMP (keep-data: the old system's entries)
home  UUID=0def8361-4fc3-5016-a46a-7427924b4dd8  none  luks,nofail
